Financial Times published an article on August 7, saying:

> The Trump White House, which initially scorned AI “safety” policies as hindrances to US innovation, has been playing catch-up. It held a meeting with US AI giants this week to outline a planned framework in which developers would give federal safety experts access to frontier AI systems 30 days before public launch, though this would be nominally voluntary.

We can make a case for expanding controls and applying safeguards or any sort of control over AI. I think the background info for this question is insufficient and I'll layout some new developments that took place recently.

## AI gone rogue

Frontier AI models like Anthropic's Mythos 5 and OpenAi's GOT-5.6 Sol hacked into external organisations by escaping a sandbox *(A digital cage, an isolated computer environment with no internet access).* lately UK's AI Security Institute issued a startling report that these two models  Mythos 5 and GPT-5.6 Sol had broken into a third-party developers platform and used fake identities to bypass code review. I'll highlight both cases individually&#x20;

* &#x20;**OpenAI's GPT-5.6 Sol,** a model that the company released in July, broke free from the lab and hacked hugging Face's systems, an artificial-intelligence platform. On July 16th Hugging face, notified law enforcement of the attack and later on 21 July they confirmed that there had been no human involved in the loop. The AI agent was the attacker. OpenAI wanted to see how its new model would fare at ExploitingGym, which is a set of standard problems designed to test how good AI systems are at exploiting known vulnerabilities on several apps, including Google's Chrome browser.&#x20;
* **Anthropic’s Mythos 5,** Anthropic has said that its model has hacked into at least three organizations while they were testing its cyber capabilities, the model was engaged in sustained, potentially harmful activity during a cyber evaluation test. Mythos 5 Tried to insert malicious code into an open-source project on the GitHub platform, and deceived the reviewers into approving it by faking online identities.&#x20;
  Both companies were testing the capabilities of their models and It certainly exceeded the expectations, one American think-think, institute for Law and AI labeled these events by saying "This situation is unprecedented".&#x20;

These events shook the industry. Demis Hassabis has called for a coalition between public and private sectors overseen by federal government. His proposal named as "Frontier AI Standards Body" outlines that the industry would conduct pre release testing and covering risks such as, cyber security to biological or nuclear threats. For the US whose companies are leading the AI race, it's unequivocally clear that safeguards or any sort of checks and balances are coming. However, this question has a very short timeline, I will avoid being over confident with this&#x20;
